@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
title Confirm dialogs — a button row, the safe answer selected, two keys to destroy
' purpose: how proveo asks a yes-or-no question on the terminal, and why Enter alone never destroys anything
' covers: internal/choiceui/dialog.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/dialog_run.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/dialog_test.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/tty_unix.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/tty_windows.go
' covers: internal/choiceui/sandbox_close.go (NewSandboxClose, SandboxCloseAnswer)
' covers: internal/choiceui/fetch_pushed.go (AskFetchPushed)
' covers: internal/choiceui/blocked_run.go (AskSandboxUpgrade, AskUnansweredRemoval)
' covers: internal/choiceui/oversized_model.go (AskOversizedModel)
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/confirm.go
' covers: e2e/close_confirm_modes_test.go (TestLiveCloseDialogStaysUpWhileTheAgentStops)
'   the decision, D7 ................ _spec/_plans/tui-upgrades-delivery.puml (DECISIONS, W5)
'   how the proxy hosts one ......... _spec/internal/ptyproxy/pty-ownership.puml
'   the stack they draw with ........ _spec/internal/choiceui/tui-stack.puml
'   sbx's own dialogs, as observed .. _spec/_experiments/sbx-tui-observed.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── WHAT IT REPLACED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Five confirmations asked for a typed answer: a field, `y` or `yes`, then
' Enter. The field looked like a place for a sandbox name, `y` alone did
' nothing, and each dialog listed its keys as rows of `[y/N]`, `[esc]`.
' The Executor, 2026-11-10, on destructive confirms (D7): "Buttons, two keys
' to destroy". Not chosen: a mnemonic that acts at once (it would make
' `sbx rm --force` one key), keeping the typed y/N, typing the sandbox name.
'
' ── THE DIALOG ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' `dialog` (dialog.go) is one Bubble Tea model for all five: the body lines,
' one row of buttons, and a key line.
'    Stay ↵    Remove    Interrupt agent
'   ←/→ move · enter accept · esc stay · ctrl+c interrupt agent
' THE SAFE ANSWER IS THE FIRST BUTTON, AND IT IS SELECTED. It carries ↵, the
' mark sbx puts on the answer Enter gives. Enter untouched, Esc, and a
' dialog's timeout all give it.
' THE OTHER ANSWER TAKES TWO KEYS. One key selects it: ←/→, Tab, its
' mnemonic letter, or `y`. Enter then accepts. No single key gives it.
'   `y` selects the first answer that is not the safe one; `n` selects the
'   safe one. So `y Enter` and `n Enter` mean what they meant in the typed
'   dialog, and the tmux driver that types them did not change.
' A DESTRUCTIVE ANSWER SHOWS ITS MNEMONIC IN THE FAILURE COLOUR, bold, inside
' its word (sbx's mnemonic letter). The letter selects; it never accepts.
' THE FIVE:
'   close this sandbox?      Stay · Remove (r, destructive) · Interrupt agent
'                            ctrl+c gives Interrupt at once: it destroys
'                            nothing and is what a second Ctrl+C means
'   the agent pushed these   Leave · Fetch (f); 15s of silence leaves them
'   created with different   Keep · Replace (r, destructive)
'     settings
'   <name> gave no answer    Keep · Remove (r, destructive)
'   <model> does not fit     Stop · Load anyway (l)
' The body copy of each is unchanged; only the answer rows went.
'
' ── WHERE A DIALOG RUNS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Four run while no agent holds the terminal. askDialog opens the operator's
' terminal itself (/dev/tty), so a redirected stdout still gets the question,
' and runs a Program on it. No terminal, or a Program that fails, answers the
' safe way and reports that nothing was asked.
' The close dialog runs INSIDE the proxy, over a live agent: the proxy hosts
' it and feeds it keys one at a time (pty-ownership.puml). Done() tells the
' proxy the dialog has its answer, so nothing typed after it is consumed.
' Package sandbox reaches the three it asks through variables
' (askFetchPushed, askSandboxUpgrade, askUnansweredRemoval); a test that
' called the real ones would open a dialog on the developer's terminal.
'
' ── THE CLOSE DIALOG STAYS UP WHILE THE AGENT STOPS (T7) ───────────────────
' After Remove the sbx client has up to 30s (stopWait) to exit. Before, the
' dialog closed at once, the agent's screen came back, and nothing said a
' stop was in flight (_spec/_experiments/terminal-handoff-probes.puml, C2).
' NewSandboxClose(stop, stopWait) now runs `stop` itself when Remove is
' accepted and stays up, with the buttons replaced by
'   stopping the agent (up to 30s) — 4s
' counting once a second. Keys are ignored. The dialog ends when the child
' exits: Run shuts the hand-off and the Program is killed. The answer is
' still Remove, and the proxy's own remove hook is no longer used.
' MEASURED in tmux on macOS and linux/arm64, with a child that takes 3s to
' stop: Enter alone handed the terminal back; `r` Enter showed the line, and
' the removal finished after it.
'
' Pinned by internal/choiceui/dialog_test.go:
'   TestEnterAloneGivesTheSafeAnswerInEveryDialog
'   TestTheOtherAnswerTakesTwoKeys
'   TestTheSelectionWrapsAndTheEnterMarkFollowsIt
'   TestTheCloseDialogSaysWhatItDoesAndNamesItsKeys
'   TestAcceptingRemoveStopsTheAgentOnceAndKeepsTheDialogUp
'   TestSilenceLeavesThePushedBranches
'   TestTheUpgradeDialogNamesTheConsequence
'   TestTheUnansweredRemovalDialogListsTheMirroredRefs
'   TestEveryDialogFitsAnEightyColumnTerminal
'   TestADangerousAnswerDrawsItsMnemonicInTheFailureColour
' and by e2e (tmux): TestLiveCloseDialogStaysUpWhileTheAgentStops,
'   TestLiveCtrlCConfirmationOnTmux, TestLivePostExitPromptTakesTheFirstKeysOnTmux,
'   TestHeadedCtrlCAsksBeforeRemoving (a fake sbx client),
'   TestCtrlCDialogOverARealAgentGivesItsScreenBackAndRemovesOnYes (real sbx)

component "the question\nbody lines, unchanged" as BODY <<app>>
component "the safe answer\nselected · carries ↵" as SAFE <<host>>
component "the other answer\none key selects, Enter accepts" as OTHER <<app>>
component "**a destructive answer**\nits mnemonic in the failure colour" as DANGER <<error>>
component "Enter · Esc · timeout" as ONE <<host>>
component "the held line\n""stopping the agent (up to 30s) — 4s""" as HOLD <<app>>

BODY ARROW_MAIN SAFE
BODY ARROW_MAIN OTHER
ONE ARROW_MAIN SAFE : one key
OTHER ARROW_OPTIONAL DANGER : when it destroys
DANGER ARROW_OPTIONAL HOLD : Remove, accepted

note bottom of SAFE
  **Enter alone never destroys.** The first
  button is the safe one and starts selected.
  Enter untouched, Esc and a timeout all give
  it, in every dialog.
end note

note bottom of OTHER
  **Two keys, never one.** An arrow, Tab, the
  mnemonic or ""y"" selects; Enter accepts. So
  ""y Enter"" still means yes, and a stray key
  cannot remove a sandbox.
end note

note bottom of HOLD
  **The stop is said while it happens.** After
  Remove the dialog stays up and counts the
  seconds until the agent's client exits, in
  place of a screen that looked untouched.
end note

note bottom of DANGER
  **The letter selects; it does not act.** A
  mnemonic that acted at once would make
  ""sbx rm --force"" one key. The Executor
  chose buttons and two keys.
end note
@enduml
